{
  "reasons": {
    "mac-command": "about the command and the sandbox settings for macOS and Linux, which Windows never builds (Windows' own: tests/windows-runs.test.js, \"Claude Code on Windows: …\")",
    "open-model": "chats with an open model are off there: no run of one has been fenced or attacked (docs/WINDOWS-PROGRESS.md)",
    "login": "logging an agent in from the panel is off there, with runs",
    "limits": "a run's limits are worked out for macOS's and Linux's sandboxes; Windows' aren't built",
    "keychain": "about the macOS Keychain and Linux's Secret Service, through stand-ins that are scripts (Credential Manager: tests/windows.test.js, tests/windows-smoke.js)",
    "setup": "about macOS's and Linux's setup: #!/bin/sh wrappers, the /usr/local/bin link, browsers' folders in the home folder (Windows' setup: tests/windows.test.js, tests/windows-smoke.js)",
    "sockets": "about Unix sockets as files (named pipes: tests/windows.test.js)",
    "legacy": "about the move from ~/.webmods (before 0.2.0), which Windows never had",
    "safari": "Safari and launchd are macOS only",
    "release": "the build and release scripts are for the Mac (zip, the Keychain)",
    "permissions": "about a folder made unreadable with chmod, which takes no reading away from its owner on Windows (no POSIX permission bits)",
    "phone-link": "the phone link and phone sync are off on Windows (no Bonjour announcer there for the phone to find the computer by: helper/lib/phonelink/link.js, docs/WINDOWS-PROGRESS.md \"Merge of phone-link\"); these tests also use keyring stand-ins that are scripts. What Windows has of it: tests/phone-link-store.test.js \"Windows: the link is off…\"",
    "modes": "about file modes (600, 700), which do nothing on Windows: the profile folder's access list keeps other users out (docs/WINDOWS.md)"
  },
